  "published": "2026-10-09T14:38:20.657Z",
  "readmeFile": "readme.md",
  "releaseNotes": "1.5.0: plural forms (value#few beside value, picked by Unicode CLDR's rules: Words.Known[key, count], and {0#key} in a template); language codes of up to three parts (zh-Hant-TW), falling back one part at a time, and a Chinese region through the script it writes (zh-TW reads zh-Hant); key names in any script, as Unicode's identifiers have them (UAX #31), in NFC; references and selectors resolve against the key they are written in; under Debug, a count whose form is missing reads its stand-in marked 🎲, and a language with no words of its own brands its first fallback too, which 1.4.0 left plain; TriggerWords, which replaces WordsTickle. Upgrading: a header that is no key name ([a b], [lang.c#]) is now skipped with a WP:NAME warning, and so is an empty [] with every field under it, which 1.4.0 read as the key above's; a file using a three-part code needs 1.5.0, as 1.4.0 drops those fields, and a code outside the grammar is now skipped; a custom IWordsParserConsumer also sees forms, as value#few fields; and a type implementing IWords (or IWordsParserConsumer) beside another interface that declares Language, UICulture, a count indexer (or VisitLine) of its own may no longer compile (CS0229, CS0121): cast to the interface you mean. IWords gains Language and UICulture, with defaults: an IWords of your own that already has a public Language or UICulture now supplies them, recompiled, so check it returns the language its words are in (Language picks the plural rules).",
